  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">AFG020N-Afghanistan forced returnee crisis ACJR</narrative>
  </title>
  <description promeva:title="Description (English)" promeva:type="104" type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CARE and WASSA aim to reach 60,877 vulnerable returnees (75%) and HC members in Islam Qala, Shabash clinic and Injil District, Herat, through an emergency response informed by the Joint RNA conducted with JR members.

FSL
-Cash for Food (Islam Qala): WASSA&amp;CARE will provide a one-time $75 cash transfer to 1,500 HHs. This CfF modality, chosen for broader reach than MPCT, was agreed with JR actors implementing MPCT. The transfer value is aligned with FSAC guidance. CfF will complement WFP distributions and coordinate with IOM referrals.
-Community Kitchens (Islam Qala and Herat): WASSA will provide hot meals to 42,900 indiv. through pre-existing, women-led community kitchens to meet immediate food needs at the border and in Herat. Meals will meet a minimum intake of 2,100 kcal per person per day.

Health
In Shabash clinic and Herat, CARE&amp; WASSA aim to reach 23,884 individuals with essential primary healthcare, including treatment of common illnesses, maternal and neonatal care, child health, immunisation, and MHPSS. The decision for CARE to implement in Shabash clinic was taken in a Health cluster meeting with all health actors implementing in IQ including JR partners. In Injil, WASSA will reactivate an emergency health facility.

Protection will be mainstreamed across the response. Measures include separate waiting areas for men and women, prioritisation of female-headed HHs, reduced waiting times for PWDs/PLWs, and tailored support for PLWs. Community mobilisers, including female staff, will share information and support referrals. PSEA and GBV awareness sessions will also be conducted.

This gender-sensitive response builds on both organisations&#x2019; contextual knowledge and aligns with FSAC, CVWG, and Health Cluster guidance and the Joint Operating Principles. CARE and WASSA will coordinate with JR partners, UN actors, authorities, and NGOs to ensure a conflict-sensitive, do-no-harm approach with strong protection and health referral pathways.</narrative>
  </description>
